\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx1008{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f1\fs44 \cf4 \b Files}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx1008{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx1008{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 This package consists of two files:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx1008{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li1872\ri0\fi-1872\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \cf1 \b LJ6DTP.DOC\tab \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 This manual}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li1872\ri0\fi-1872\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \cf1 \b LJ6DTP.PDF\tab \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 A specially modified WordStar PDF}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f1\fs44 \cf4 \b Using LJ6DTP.PDF}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 This PDF can be used with WordStar 6.0 and 7.0, but only modified with WordStar 7.0 Revision D. It should work well with any printer that emulates the HP LaserJet 4, LaserJet 5, LaserJet 6, or greater printers, and for printing to disk for conversion to Adobe Acrobat PDF (Portable Document Format) files via \b PrintFilePrinter\b0  (which you'll find in C:\\WS\\PrintFilePrinter). Print out this document, then compare the contents of this file to the printout to see how the effects were accomplished.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \page \f3\fs24 \cf1 By default, WordStar is set up to use the PC-8 IBM extended-character set. Unfortunately, the single open quote (reverse apostrophe) and single close quote (apostrophe) characters in the PC-8 symbol set are truly ugly. The open quote is a diagonal straight line and the close quote is a vertical straight line. This makes it impossible to double these characters up to make proper double open and close quotes.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 However, the LaserJet 4 and above printers contain several symbol sets, including some that do have attractive single open and close quotes that are mirror images of each other\~_ ideal for making proper quotation marks. For instance, in the DeskTop symbol set, these characters look like this:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 `}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 '}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 WordStar's PDFEDIT (Printer Description File editor) allows you to mix and match characters from various symbol sets. In the accompanying PDF, we've replaced the ugly PC-8 single quotes with the elegant ones from the DeskTop symbol set. (Except for the modifications described herein, this is simply a standard WordStar 7.0 LaserJet 4P PDF.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Unfortunately, you can't just swap one character for another and expect right justification to still work. You also have to patch the proportional spacing tables for the PDF to use the appropriate values from the other symbol set. We've done this as well in this PDF.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Finally, to be able to double up these single quote characters to get attractive double quotes, the pairs should be kerned (tucked closer together), so we've also added these two pairs to the PDF kerning tables:}\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ``}\par
\pard\plain\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ''}\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Now, when kerning is on, which is the default (although you can set it specifically to on or off with the .KR dot command, as I did for the examples below), those pairs will look great:}\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx1872{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab ``These quotation marks are too loose because kerning is turned off.''}\par
\pard\plain\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab ``But these quotation marks are just right because kerning is turned on.''}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Using the same techniques of (1) character-set translation, (2) character substitution, and (3) adjusting proportional-spacing tables, we've added several other characters to this PDF that are useful for desktop publishing:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \page \f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u9787?\tab copyright symbol (some fonts only)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u9788?\tab ellipsis points}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab _\tab em dash (long dash)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u8801?\tab en dash (short dash)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u171?\tab double open quotes (individual character)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u187?\tab double close quotes (individual character)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \tab \u9829?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab upper-left rounded box corner (Univers only)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \tab \u9830?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab upper-right rounded box corner (Univers only)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \tab \u9827?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab lower-left rounded box corner (Univers only)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs24 \tab \u9824?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab lower-right rounded box corner (Univers only)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Note that we've only made these substitutions in the proportional fonts; we've left the fixed-width fonts Courier, Letter Gothic, and Line Printer unmodified.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 You can use either two single quotes or one double quote character, but I don't recommend mixing them in the same document, since the spacing between the two single quotes might not exactly match that between the two components of the double-quote character.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 The copyright symbol comes from the PS Math symbol set. That symbol set actually contains two different copyright symbols. Hex character E3 has a sans-serif \i C\i0  in the center of the circle, like this: \f2\fs24 \u9787?\f3\fs24 \cf1 .  We've mapped that one into the Arial, Omega, and Univers fonts. And hex character D3 has a serif \i C\i0  in the center of the circle, like this: \u9787?. We've mapped that one into the Times and Times New Roman fonts. \b \i Note:\b0 \i0  The use of the copyright symbol is only supported in the following typeface families: Arial, Omega, Times, Times Roman, and Univers (we've mapped a copyright symbol to character position 02 in all the typefaces, but WordStar will not accept a proportional-spacing value there in the other typefaces).}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 To access the special characters in LJ6DTP.PDF refer to the following chart.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx720{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx2160\tx5472{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \b You type\tab Shows on screen as\tab Prints as\b0 \f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx2160\tx5472{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P02<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9787?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u9787?\tab copyright symbol}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P03<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9829?\f2\fs24 \tab \u9829?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab upper-left rounded box corner}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P04<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9830?\f2\fs24 \tab \u9830?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab upper-right rounded box corner}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P05<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9827?\f2\fs24 \tab \u9827?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab lower-left rounded box corner}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P06<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9824?\f2\fs24 \tab \u9824?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab lower-right rounded box corner}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P015<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u9788?\f2\fs24 \tab \u9788?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab ellipsis points}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f0\fs24 '\f3\fs24 \cf1  (apostrophe)\tab '\tab '\tab single close quote}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f0\fs24 _\f3\fs24 \cf1  (underscore)\tab \f0\fs24 _\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab _\tab em dash}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f0\fs24 `\f3\fs24 \cf1  (single open quote)\tab `\tab `\tab single open quote}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P0174<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u171?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u171?\tab double open quote}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P0175<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u187?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u187?\tab double close quote}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 ^P0240<Enter>\tab \f0\fs24 \u8801?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \tab \u8801?\tab en dash}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \page Note that WordStar's Advanced Page Preview knows nothing about these character substitutions, so you won't see the special characters until you actually print your document.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f1\fs44 \cf4 \b Kerning Lowercase Letter Pairs}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 As distributed, WordStar's WINSTALL.EXE only installs kerning values greater than \u177?7, which means no lowercase letter pairs (such as \u171?wo\u187?) get kerned. Kerning tables take up a lot of room in the PDF, which is why WordStar sets the threshold value so high. But for three typeface families _ Optima, Times, and Univers _ we've installed all kerning values greater than \u177?2. Use these typefaces when you want kerning of lowercase letter pairs.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 \f1\fs44 \cf4 \b That Box Around The Front Page}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 Hey! How, you might ask, did I get that nifty box outline around these pages? That was done using WordStar custom print controls (of the type created with the ^P! command) that contain code in Hewlett-Packard's PCL printer-control language. The tag is included on each page of this document; the first one is on page 1, line 11).}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\tx3312\tx5472\tx6048{\f3\fs24 \cf1 The tag was produced with \b LSRBOX\b0  utility, which I also wrote. You'll find it in \b C:\\WS\\LSRBOX\b0  (and the documentation there will show you some super-powerful things you can do with WordStar's merge-print capability _ WordStar \i invented\i0  merge printing). LSRBOX can do a lot of things with lines and boxes, such as creating the racing-stripe design below:}\par
