\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f1\fs44 \cf4 \b \f3\fs24 \cf1 \b0 To print white text on a black background, first you have to make the black background. From the ^P- menu, select \u171?Black.\u187? Then select a font with which to draw the background. The font used for the background should be of a bigger point size than the text that will appear in the foreground. You'll have to experiment a bit to get it just right, but I find that a font two points larger than what you plan to use for the white text is usually a good starting place.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Use character decimal 219 (the solid block, \f0\fs24 \u9608?\f3\fs24 \cf1 \~) to draw the background. You can enter this character from WordStar's ^P0 Extended Character Menu. (If you plan to do a lot of white-on-black text, you may find it simplest to program a function key to issue this character.) Draw the background by repeatedly entering this character (or enter it once, delete it with ^T, then repeatedly unerase it with ^U). Note the inches indicator in the status line _ it will tell you how long your background will be. Now, issue WordStar's Overprint line command (^PM). If you've done this correctly, a \u171?-\u187? will show in the flag column.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f3\fs24 \cf1 Now select \u171?White\u187? from the ^P- menu. Select an appropriate font for your text (remember, it should be a smaller point size than what you used for the background) and type away. When finished, select \u171?Black\u187? from the ^P- menu (otherwise the rest of your text will be invisible _ white letters on a white background). You may want to adjust the background length, or center both the background and foreground, like so:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\tx3744\tx7200{\f3\fs24 \cf1 }\par
